Description: Use dpkg-divert --no-rename for start-stop-daemon diversion
 chroot-setup.sh's divert() / undivert() helpers run
 `dpkg-divert --rename` against /sbin/start-stop-daemon, which is
 shipped by the dpkg package (Essential).  Modern dpkg (>= 1.19) warns
 on every such invocation:
 .
   dpkg-divert: warning: diverting file '/sbin/start-stop-daemon' from
   an Essential package with rename is dangerous, use --no-rename
 .
 The 2026-05-13 reference install log shows ~23 of these warnings
 spread across base-installer, apt-setup, and finish-install
 invocations — each step that calls chroot_setup logs one.  None are
 functional issues but they're noise that drowns out real warnings
 in operator log review.
 .
 Switch both call sites to --no-rename.  This means the Essential
 file isn't physically renamed (just shadowed by the diversion at
 lookup time), which matches what dpkg's own maintainer scripts do
 when they need to divert files from Essential packages.  The
 functional behaviour during install is identical — chroot_setup
 still routes start-stop-daemon calls to the .REAL stub that does
 nothing during install, so daemon start attempts during dpkg
 unpack/configure are still suppressed; only the rename machinery
 differs.
Author: Harkirat S Virk <harkiratsvirk@gmail.com>
Origin: local
Forwarded: no
Last-Update: 2026-05-13
---
This patch header follows DEP-3: http://dep.debian.net/deps/dep3/
--- a/chroot-setup.sh
+++ b/chroot-setup.sh
@@ -26,12 +26,12 @@ mountpoints () {
 }

 divert () {
-	chroot /target dpkg-divert --quiet --add --divert "$1.REAL" --rename "$1"
+	chroot /target dpkg-divert --quiet --add --divert "$1.REAL" --no-rename "$1"
 }

 undivert () {
 	rm -f "/target$1"
-	chroot /target dpkg-divert --quiet --remove --rename "$1"
+	chroot /target dpkg-divert --quiet --remove --no-rename "$1"
 }

 chroot_setup () {
